The market (equilibrium) price is the price at which the quantity demanded equals the quantity supplied, so that there is neither a surplus nor a shortage and no pressure on the price to change.

**(i) Before the application of fertilizer.** Compare the demand column with the supply column "before":

- at 70 (000s FCFA): demand 180 and supply 340, a surplus of $340 - 180 = 160$ tons, so sellers cut the price;
- at 50: demand 300 and supply 140, a shortage of $300 - 140 = 160$ tons, so buyers bid the price up;
- at **60**: demand = supply = 240 tons.

**The market price is 60 thousand FCFA (60 000 FCFA) per ton, and 240 tons are bought and sold.** It is the only price at which the plans of buyers and sellers match, so there is no surplus or shortage to push the price up or down.

**(ii) After the application of fertilizer.** Compare the demand column with the supply column "after":

- at 60: demand 240 and supply 480, a surplus of $480 - 240 = 240$ tons, so the price falls;
- at 50: demand 300 and supply 430, a surplus of 130 tons, so the price still falls;
- at 30: demand 360 and supply 270, a shortage of 90 tons, so the price would rise;
- at **40**: demand = supply = 340 tons.

**The new market price is 40 thousand FCFA (40 000 FCFA) per ton, and 340 tons are traded.** Fertilizer raised yields and shifted the supply curve to the right (supply increased), so the equilibrium price fell from 60 to 40 and the equilibrium quantity rose from 240 to 340 tons.

